.TH PLOT 3G
.SH NAME
openpl \fIet al.\fR \- graphics interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
Standard Unix subroutines
.PP
.nf
.ta 2.2i
\fBopenpl( )\fR
\fBerase( )\fR
\fBspace(x0, y0, x1, y1)\fR	int x0, y0, x1, y1;
\fBlabel(string)\fR	char string[ ];
\fBline(x0, y0, x1, y1)\fR	int x0, y0, x1, y1;
\fBcircle(x, y, rad)\fR	int x, y, rad;
\fBarc(x, y, x0, y0, x1, y1)\fR	int x, y, x0, y0, x1, y1;
\fBmove(x, y)\fR	int x, y;
\fBcont(x, y)\fR	int x, y;
\fBpoint(x, y)\fR	int x, y;
\fBlinemod(style)\fR	char style[ ];
\fBclosepl( )\fR
.fi
.PP
Special VENIX Enhancements
.PP
.nf
.ta 2.2i
\fBlinepat(pat)\fR	int pat;
\fBlinewid(width)\fR	int width;
\fBwindow(x0, y0, x1, y1)\fR	int x0, y0, x1, y1;
\fBbox(x0, y0, x1, y1)\fR	int x0, y0, x1, y1;
\fBrfill(x0, y0, x1, y1)\fR	int x0, y0, x1, y1;
\fBfill(x, y)\fR	int x, y;
\fBdot(x, y, rad)\fR	int x, y, rad;
\fBcolor(col)\fR	int col;
\fBcolndx(col, pat)\fR	int col, pat;
\fBwritemod(s)\fR	char s[ ];
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These subroutines generate graphic output in a relatively device-independent
manner.
.B openpl()
must be used before any of the others to
open the device for graphics.
.B closepl()
flushes the output and closes the
device.
.PP
String arguments to
.BR label() ,
.BR linemod() ,
and
.B writemod()
are null-terminated,
and do not contain newlines.
.PP
All coordinate points used in the routines are user-coordinates, defined
by the
.B space()
subroutine.
.B space()
must be called to set up this coordinate
system, or you may get strange results!
.PP
The last designated point in a call to
.BR line() ,
.BR move() ,
.BR cont() ,
or
.BR point() ,
becomes the `current point' for the next plotting instruction.
.PP
Standard Unix subroutines
.RS
.TP
.B openpl( )
Initialize graphics output device for writing.
The routine
will return a zero value upon a successful initialization.
If an error is encountered, the routine will return a value
of \-1.
.TP
.B erase( )
Erase the graphics screen.
.TP
\fBspace(\fIx0, y0, x1, y1\fB)\fR
Set up plotting area.
The coordinate points (x0,y0) and
(x1,y1) specify respectively the lower-left and upper-right
corners of the user-coordinate system to be defined.
The
user-coordinate system is scaled to fit the largest possible
square region allowable on the graphics device screen.
All plotting instructions are produced with respect to the
user-coordinate system.
.br
The upper limits defined are just outside the plotting
area.
.br
Video screens which are not square will display a blank
portion outside the plotting area.
You may plot beyond
the space settings in order to take advantage of this area.
.TP
\fBlabel(\fIs\fB)\fR
Write out the ASCII text string so that its first character
falls on the current point.
.TP
\fBline(\fIx0, y0, x1, y1\fB)\fR
Draw a line from (x0,y0) to (x1,y1).
(x1,y1) becomes the
new current point.
.TP
\fBcircle(\fIx, y, r\fB)\fR
Draw a circle with center at (x,y) having radius r.
.TP
\fBarc(\fIx, y, x0, y0, x1, y1\fB)\fR
Draw an arc with center at (x,y).
The next two points
determine the starting and ending octants (inclusive and
exclusive) for a counter-clockwise arc.
The point (x0,y0)
is referenced for arc radius.
.TP
\fBmove(\fIx, y\fB)\fR
Move the current point to (x,y).
.TP
\fBcont(\fIx, y\fB)\fR
Draw from current point to (x,y).
(x,y) becomes the new
current point.
.TP
\fBpoint(\fIx, y\fB)\fR
Draw a point at (x,y).
(x,y) becomes the new current
point.
.TP
\fBlinemod(\fIs\fB)\fR
Change line-style attribute for subsequent lines.
Styles
available are: `solid', `dotted', `longdashed', `shortdashed',
`dotdashed'.
.TP
.B closepl( )
Close graphics output device for writing.
.RE
.PP
Special VENIX Enhancements
.RS
.TP
\fBlinepat(\fIpattern\fB)\fR
Specify a 16-bit integer bit pattern, of user's choice, to
be used in place of one of the available line styles.
Bits
set to `1' are visible; bits set to `0' are invisible.
.br
EX: The integer
.I 0146314
in octal represents
.I `1100110011001100'
which is a dashed line.
.TP
\fBlinewid(\fIwidth\fB)\fR
Specify line width in user coordinates.
.TP
\fBwindow(\fIx0, y0, x1, y1\fB)\fR
The points (x0,y0) and (x1,y1) specify respectively the
lower-left and upper-right corners of a clipping window
in user coordinates.
Only portions of lines inside the
window will be displayed, and all erasures will be
confined to the window area only.
.TP
\fBbox(\fIx0, y0, x1, y1\fB)\fR
Draw a box frame bounded by the lower-left and upper-right
points (x0,y0) and (x1,y1).
.TP
\fBrfill(\fIx0, y0, x1, y1\fB)\fR
Draw a filled rectangle bounded by the user coordinates
(x0,y0) and (x1,y1).
The fill pattern is determined by the
current line-style pattern (see
.BR linepat() ).
.TP
\fBfill(\fIx, y\fB)\fR
Fill a convex closed boundary of arbitrary shape.
The
point (x,y) is the seed point (starting point) and must be
inside the boundary.
The fill pattern is determined by the
current line-style pattern (see
.BR linepat() ).
.TP
\fBdot(\fIx, y, r\fB)\fR
Draw a filled circle with center at (x,y), having a radius
r.
The fill pattern is determined by the current line-style
pattern (see
.BR linepat() ).
.TP
\fBcolor(\fIc\fB)\fR
Choose a color from the present color palette.
All graphics
following will be displayed in that color.
The color
palette is preset as follows:
.sp
.nf
.ta .8i 1.8i
	color	shade
.sp
	0	black (background)
	1	blue
	2	green
	3	cyan
	4	red
	5	magenta
	6	yellow
	7	white
.fi
.sp
The
.B colndx()
routine below explains how to change the
color palette.
On a monochrome monitor, the colors
will show up as gray scales.
Be cautious, as this routine
.I "should not"
be called unless an extended bit map board
(color board) is present in the machine.
.TP
\fBcolndx(\fIc, pat\fB)\fR
Set a color from the palette to a desired shade.
In the
DEC PRO color graphics, the colors available are numbered
0 thru 7, 0 being the background color.
The pattern
argument is a 16-bit integer to be set by the user.
.sp
.nf
.ta .5i 2.4i
	15\-\-8	7\-\-0
.sp
	0 ... 0	r r r g g g b b
.fi
.sp
The high 8 bits are ignored and should be set to zero.
The low 8 bits specify the individual intensity of the
three primary colors: red, green, and blue.
Intensities for
red and green may range from 0 to 7, and blue from 0 to
3.
For example, a pattern of
.I `034'
in octal represents
.I "`00000000 000 111 00'"
in binary, which is a full-intensity
green.
.sp
Be cautious, as a change in the color palette will instantly change
that color on the screen.
It is advised that this routine be called
before any plotting to eliminate color flashing.
This may be useful,
however, for creating special effects for demo purposes.
.I "Do not"
use this routine unless an extended bit map board (color
board) is present in the machine.
.TP
\fBwritemod(\fIs\fB)\fR
Choose from one of the five available writing modes:
.RS
.TP 1i
.B xor
.I Exclusive-or
mode allows one to overlay
several images onto the same screen, and
remove them arbitrarily while retaining the
underlying image.
.br
[exclusive `OR' data to screen: memory ^= data]
.TP
.B mov
.I Absolute-move
mode overwrites anything on
the screen and is good for clearing off previous
images.
.br
[move data to screen: memory = data]
.TP
.B mvc
.I Move-complement
mode overwrites the
screen with a reverse image, creating reverse-video
effects.
.br
[move complement of data to screen:
memory = ~data]
.TP
.B bis
.I Bit-set
mode writes only the set (turned-on)
bits onto the screen.
The current image is
not destroyed, and thus this mode is useful
for creating composite images.
.br
[`OR' data to screen: memory |= data]
.TP
.B bic
.I Bit-clear
mode writes only the clear (turned-off)
bits onto the screen.
.br
[`AND' complement of data to screen:
memory &= ~data]
.RE
.sp
All plotting instructions operate in the chosen writing mode.
.br
.I Bit-set
mode is the default.
.RE
.PP
Various flavors of these functions exist for different output devices.
They
are obtained by the following
.BR ld (1)
options (normally placed at the end
of the line in the
.B cc
command):
.TP 8
.B \-lplot
device-independent graphics stream on standard output for
.BR plot (1g)
filters
.PP
The following options bypass the plot filters and write directly to the device:
.TP 8
.B \-ltpro
DEC PRO monochrome and color graphics screens
.br
Color is distinguished from monochrome by a first subroutine
call to
.BR color() .
If you want monochrome output, do
not make any calls to either
.B color()
or
.BR colndx() .
Color will
work only if an extended bit map board (color board) is
present in the machine.
If your machine has a monochrome
monitor
.I and
a color board, use of the color subroutines will
produce gray scales.
.TP
.B \-lt4014
Tektronix 4014 terminal
.SH EXAMPLES
To make a program using the device independent plot library:
.IP
cc \-o program program.c \-lplot
.PP
To run the program on the DEC PRO graphics screen:
.IP
program < data | plot \-Tpro
.PP
To run the program on a Tektronix terminal:
.IP
program < data | plot \-T4014 > /dev/tek
.PP
To bypass the plot filters and write directly to the DEC PRO:
.IP
.nf
cc \-o program program.c \-ltpro
program < data
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
plot(1g), plot(5), setscreen(1g)
.SH NOTES
The
.B fill()
routine operates a boundary-fill operation and requires an
unbroken boundary in order to fill properly.
Please be cautious when
using it.
.sp
